Well, the facts are that such people do exist and they have exercised the same rights as all of us have when we are confronted with unwanted emotions: they have sought help. The practices and techniques used have come to be loosely referred to as ‘conversion therapy’
While the document (PHPJ) purports to paint an accurate picture of LGBT ‘conversion therapy’ it is a deeply flawed document at many levels. It is flawed in its basic design, it is flawed in methodology and therefore its conclusions cannot help but be flawed.
In fact, the only reference given by the authors is a position statement from the Australian Psychological Society 2015 ‘APS Position Statement on the use of psychological practices that attempt to change sexual orientation’. It makes the following strong and clear statements:
Again, you would expect strong, comprehensive claims to be backed up by strong comprehensive evidence. When you examine the references provided, there is only one study by Shidio & Schroeder from 2002 which itself is a ‘consumer report’.
Should government policy be based on ‘We just don’t know’? Should therapies, where there is at least anecdotal evidence that thousands of people around the world have benefited without harm, be criminalised based on ‘We just don’t know’?
So in summing up, the authors have engaged in a process of circular reasoning.
