Then there are some of us for whom smoking meat satisfies a primal need. Regardless of where you fall on this spectrum, there’s no denying that smoking meat requires a fair amount of concentration and skill that makes you feel accomplished at the end of each meal.
However, it doesn’t have to be all that difficult if you use the right charcoal grill smoker. Modern-day charcoal smokers can maintain the same temperature for several hours which means you can spend more time marinating the next batch or catching up on the latest issue of Smoke Signals Magazine.
For me, as a BBQ lover, the charcoal smoker is the favorite type of smoker. It offers all that I love about smoking and that gives me lots of fun.
When looking for a new charcoal smoker, you may run into many problems and questions that you will find the answers to after reading this guide. There are many types of charcoal smokers so to make the task easier for you, I have prepared a list of the best charcoal smokers out of each category.
10 Tips for buying a new charcoal smoker
As the name implies, a charcoal smoker is powered by charcoal and you use it to smoke food at low temperatures. This means you have to slow cook the food for long periods of time, but this often leads to meat that’s tender and more flavorful, especially with the smokiness coming through.
A charcoal smoker is also easy to control once you get the hang of it, and it doesn’t require extra equipment like the other types of smokers. Cooking meat with fire can also have a nostalgic feel to it as it reminds us of how our ancestors used to cook and commune.
A charcoal smoker is more demanding of your time than other models because you have to watch the smoker to make sure that it’s heated properly. This requires cautious tending, especially in the beginning, otherwise, too much heat can ruin your meal.
What You Should Look for in the Best Charcoal Smoker in 2019?
So, what sets the charcoal smoker apart from the rest?
If you’re looking for something compact that you can use on the go then a smaller sized grill will do. But, if you’re looking for something that will allow you to cater to large groups of people then you’ll need a large sized grill.
Portability is another important factor to keep in mind when looking for a charcoal smoker. Obviously, a hefty grill is great to use when catering for a large number of people but it’s very difficult to move from one place to another.
While some people view modern features as superfluous extras, others see them as worthy additions that make your smoker even more awesome. For instance, it’s important for your smoker to have an integrated thermometer so you don’t have to buy it separately because it’s an essential component.
The only thing the smoker exposes your meat to is smoke and heated air. That’s why it takes longer to cook but the results are intensified flavor and smokiness.
It’s possible to convert a grill into a smoker but not the other way around. You see, some grills come with two cooking surfaces which means you can place the meat on one end of the grill while heating the other side.
Leave the top exhaust damper open to make sure the fire burns clean. You need to keep the oxygen flowing to avoid the build-up of residue which can cause creosote and that won’t taste nice on your meat.
Leaving them in the smoker after use will degrade the material and lead to rusting over time. Just wait for the smoker to cool down first before you throw out the ashes so you don’t burn yourself.
This is the easiest area to clean and you don’t have to do it after each use, but once after every few times, you use the smoker. All you need to do is take a knife or the sharp edge of a spoon and scrape off any crust that may have adhered onto the surface.
It requires that you pay attention to the weather conditions outside too, as that may affect the speed at which the fire heats up, and time-consuming preparatory tasks like this might be annoying to some. To others, it’s all a part of the beautiful ritual of getting perfectly smoked meat.
